WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:04.963
What we wear says a lot about who we are.

00:00:04.963 --> 00:00:09.010
Yet fashion is also a
$2.5 trillion industry

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.103
that touches everyone on Earth.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.250
I'm Imran Amed.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.890
I first started trying to make
sense of the fashion business

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:21.020
15 years ago, as it was being
transformed by technology,

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:24.540
globalization, and
shifting consumer values.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.368
And so stuff like this would
just end up in a landfill.

00:00:27.368 --> 00:00:28.584
Yeah.

00:00:28.584 --> 00:00:31.542
Now I'm on a journey to see
how fashion is recalibrating

00:00:31.542 --> 00:00:35.895
after the pandemic to
balance profit with purpose.

00:00:35.895 --> 00:00:38.120
This is the Business of Fashion Show.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:42.080
Join me to discover how fashion
shapes, business, culture,

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.330
and identity,

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:46.263
and to meet the people
forging fashion's future.

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:54.180
Well, I'm back here in Paris

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.360
for the first time in 16 months.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.960
And it's the first time the
global fashion community

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:04.713
is really getting
together since March 2020.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:08.530
It's Haute Couture Week,
the most exclusive,

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:11.460
most elaborate and most creative event

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:12.913
on the fashion calendar.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.070
This is a world of rules and traditions,

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:20.280
of exquisite workmanship
and high net worth clients

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.500
able to pay six figures for a gown.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.090
Do you buy couture?

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:24.923
Absolutely.

00:01:24.923 --> 00:01:25.756
Yeah.

00:01:25.756 --> 00:01:28.230
Yet the trailblazing
designers we're about to meet

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:29.993
may not be what you expect.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.990
Among them, a true disruptor in fashion,

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.710
the man who made a billion dollar industry

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:38.320
out of ugly sneakers

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.920
is staging Balenciaga's first couture show

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.137
in over 50 years.

00:01:42.137 --> 00:01:43.410
How are you?

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.380
We had an exclusive preview.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:48.370
Couture, for me, has something very modern

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:51.460
in its concept because we only
produce what somebody orders.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.280
This is like the most
sustainable way of consuming.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.360
And the rising star of Paris,

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.380
whose highly creative
collections placed the planet

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:02.090
before profit, embedding sustainability

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:03.700
into her design process.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.970
We really have to think about the planet

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:07.650
that we are destroying every day,

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.920
so I was hoping that maybe, you know,

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.910
some other people of the fashion industry

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.560
were thinking about that.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.300
But to really understand what's happening

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.810
in Paris this week,

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:20.323
it's important to take
stock of the last 18 months.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.750
2020 was the worst year in
the history of modern luxury.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:31.090
Before that, annual revenues
for the luxury fashion market

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.386
topped $380 billion.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.260
More than two thirds of growth was buoyed

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:42.260
by the Chinese, at home, but
especially as tourists abroad.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:45.350
The lion's share of revenues
came from accessories,

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:48.873
especially handbags, with
designer sneakers catching up.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.160
Then, Wuhan, China locked down.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.250
Covid hit Europe during the
2020 spring fashion show season.

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:00.140
Giorgio Armani livestreamed his show

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:02.550
from an empty theater in Milan,

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.480
but other brands forged on,

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.253
with shows packed full
of fashion insiders.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.120
In a global business so interconnected,

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.530
unforeseen consequences of
the pandemic came to light.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:17.120
Delayed weddings led to
canceled embroidery orders

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:18.400
out of Paris,

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.680
threatening the livelihoods

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.254
of the Karigar artisans of India.

00:03:22.254 --> 00:03:25.670
Luxury revenues plunged by more than 20%.

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:27.440
Companies cut costs.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.330
But by the end of 2020,

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:33.043
Covid-19 had still devastated
industry profitability.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:37.410
Fashion was forced to adapt,

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:41.260
using technology to drive
creative and business innovation.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:42.980
The fashion business was re-imagined

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:46.823
through distance selling, online
films and virtual fashion.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.120
The luxury industry bounced back,

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.660
dominated by the mega
brands, Hermes and Chanel,

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.953
and the mega groups, LVMH,
Kering and Richemont.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:01.980
By the first half of 2021

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.720
revenues for some of the biggest names

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:07.220
were up as much as 40%
on pre-pandemic levels

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.003
delivering record profits.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:12.070
From an economic perspective,

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:15.130
these super winners are
emerging from the pandemic

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:16.233
even stronger.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.810
Now, there is a reawakening in Paris,

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.780
the global capital of luxury fashion.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.700
It's a historical echo.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:28.630
The last time fashion
burst back to life here

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:30.350
was after World War Two,

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:34.800
with the sensation that was
Christian Dior's "New Look".

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.920
It will take more than a new silhouette

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.770
to transform the fashion industry now.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:42.730
I mean, the big question
for me is, really,

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:44.790
where does the industry go from here?

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:47.010
And you know, how does it
need to adapt and change

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:50.490
to be kind of fit for this new world

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:51.490
that we're entering?

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.920
To find out more about this reawakening,

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:56.530
I met with a fashion insider

00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:00.093
with his finger on the creative
pulse of Parisian fashion.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:01.793
Pierre!

00:05:01.793 --> 00:05:03.380
Hey Imran, how are you?

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:04.350
Good, how are you?

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:05.430
I'm good.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:08.980
Pierre Alexandre M'Pelé,
also known as Pam Boy,

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:10.830
caught the ear of fashion insiders

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.580
for his honest online fashion critiques.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:14.630
Is that an earring?

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:18.290
Yeah, it's Alan Crocetti,
it's like a little rose.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:21.490
I was like, fashion moment.

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:24.730
Now, he collaborates with
the respected fashion editor,

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:26.930
Katie Grand, at Perfect,

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:30.303
a fashion media company
founded amid the pandemic.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.880
What is La Perle, like, why
did you pick this place?

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:35.170
Who comes here?

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:39.310
Everyone, it's you know,
designers and, you know,

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:41.170
we always joke about, you know,

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:42.590
dress well and come to La Perle

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:45.090
and you'll get an internship
or you'll find a job.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.920
In the 1960s or '70s,

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:52.900
there was no luxury industry, you know?

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:56.560
But now luxury is this global industry,

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.190
and what's the role of luxury?

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:00.240
I mean, I think the role of luxury

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:03.540
is to preserve craftsmanship.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:08.260
We tend to, you know, look
down on manual work and craft,

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:09.960
and I think luxury,

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:12.470
if anything, has always been about that.

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:15.710
Going back to Louis XIV in France

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:17.640
and how he developed, you know,

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.933
the French fashion industry
as we know it today.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:24.800
As you think about the fashion industry

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:26.970
we want to create for the future,

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:30.080
do you feel like there's
actually real change happening

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:31.970
or is it at the surface?

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:35.380
I mean, I feel like changing
the structures of power

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:38.160
in the industry and how they interconnect,

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.460
I feel like companies need to take risks.

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:42.250
They need diversity and inclusion.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:44.020
I feel like fashion is
a global conversation.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:45.920
Everyone is concerned, you know.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:47.260
I don't think that would have happened

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:48.820
if it weren't for the pandemic.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:51.240
I mean, Paris felt weird, you know,

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.330
between the pandemic and
the whole conversation

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:54.163
of Black Lives Matter,

00:06:54.163 --> 00:06:56.490
which was something very new in France,

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:58.410
I feel like as soon as we started talking

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.380
about systemic racism in the industry,

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:03.200
a lot of people reached out
to me and came to realize that

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:07.450
fashion is also about how
do we want society to be,

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:10.250
for example, in the '60s
with the sexual revolution,

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:12.380
and women started wearing miniskirt,

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:15.610
that was fashion changing
society and shaping society,

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:19.690
and you know, pushing
new ideas about womanhood

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:21.330
and what a woman could be,

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:22.570
and now with menswear,

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:25.030
there's been a lot of
conversation about masculinity,

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:26.460
what it means to be a man,

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:30.480
and I think that it's questions
that fashion can answer

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.810
fashion is probably the best, you know,

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:35.050
medium to answer those questions.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:37.340
I always say, it's time
to open the windows.

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:42.340
We need a fresh breeze of
newness to kind of, you know,

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:44.633
swipe through to the industry.

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:50.420
It really does feel like
change is the watchword here.

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:53.350
As I caught up with top
designers and creatives

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:55.620
in town for Paris Fashion Week.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:57.160
You know, everyone was
talking about a reset,

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:59.250
are we going back to where we were?

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:02.340
I hope not personally, but I
mean, change is a subtle one.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:03.480
I mean, the truth of the matter is,

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:07.717
we've got centuries and
decades of this fashion world

00:08:07.717 --> 00:08:11.510
and fashion business and
hopefully the mentalities evolve.

00:08:11.510 --> 00:08:13.140
And I don't think we
should go back completely

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:14.113
to where we were.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:17.190
There is like, it's a big movement

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:20.480
going into that opening
of different gender

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.260
and also races, all that opening.

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:25.503
The majority are for the opening,

00:08:25.503 --> 00:08:28.690
and the minority is the
ones that are against it.

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:29.743
That is a big change.

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:44.060
I think that the clientele
in the past two years,

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:48.100
it's gone from being a very
Western clientele actually,

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:51.540
and one really rooted and
based in the United States,

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.103
to being much more global.

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:01.040
Creativity needs to be
balanced with business.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:04.700
The most crucial decision
any fashion CEO makes

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:06.610
is the selection of a creative director

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:10.110
who can inform the entire
universe of the brand,

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:12.470
not just the collections and the clothes,

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:15.300
but also the stores, the advertising,

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:18.523
and in the digital age,
the social media strategy.

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:22.210
The legendary designer Karl Lagerfeld

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:26.130
was creative director at Fendi
for more than half a century

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:28.670
and had a lifelong contract with Chanel,

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:32.360
where he worked until he died in 2019.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:35.680
But more recently, designers
have been switching sides

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:37.180
at a fast clip,

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:40.050
akin to soccer managers in transfer season

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:42.000
netting the right striker.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.450
Although in this game,

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:46.670
when talents transfer
between historic houses,

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:49.830
it's known to insiders as musical chairs.

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:52.400
When Balenciaga's parent company Kering

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.870
acquired the brand in 2001,

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:59.290
it was doing only $17.3
million in revenue.

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:01.660
That has all changed.

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:04.870
Analysts say it's doing almost
2 billion Euros in sales,

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:06.450
which makes it one of the biggest,

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:08.833
most important fashion
houses in the world.

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:11.530
A large part of this success

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:14.960
is down to its creative
director, Demna Gvasalia,

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:18.150
who has injected his dystopian
vision into the brand,

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.840
with experience honed at the
Antwerp Academy of Fine Arts,

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.723
Maison Martin Margiela, and Louis Vuitton.

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:29.480
From Georgian refugee to
master fashion disruptor,

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.360
Demna is part of a group of designers

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.350
including Virgil Abloh and Kim Jones

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:36.120
who brought the streetwear phenomenon

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:39.020
to the highest levels of European fashion.

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:40.160
He's the creative mind

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:42.730
behind Balenciaga's first couture show

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:44.373
in more than 50 years.

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.160
This is the 50th couture collection,

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:51.600
because Mr. Balenciaga
himself did 49 collections.

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:53.250
So this is the 50th collection,

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:57.270
it's Demna's first couture collection.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:10:59.740
And so it's probably the most important,

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:03.550
most anticipated moment
of this Couture Week,

00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:05.500
and we're getting the first look at it.

00:11:08.525 --> 00:11:09.913
It's like the gate to couture.

00:11:09.913 --> 00:11:10.803
That's correct.

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:20.840
So, they're about to start the fittings.

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:23.940
They have to allocate which
model is gonna wear which look.

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:26.070
But now the fittings
have also become a moment

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:28.090
to capture content to share later.

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:31.945
So there's a whole
photograph setup as well.

00:11:31.945 --> 00:11:33.780
And you can just tell
by the energy in here,

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:36.940
there's lots of anticipation
because some of the looks

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:38.880
are being revealed for the first time.

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:40.410
Nobody in the whole world

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:43.130
outside this building
has seen this collection.

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:46.280
An emigre himself,

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:49.510
Cristobal Balenciaga
shaped the fashion scene

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:51.780
through the 1940s and '50s,

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:54.280
with his sculptural masterpieces,

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:57.400
until he shuttered the house in 1968.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:58.430
How are you feeling?

00:11:58.430 --> 00:11:59.630
Good, very excited,

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:01.353
now it's the moment
actually, the hardest one,

00:12:01.353 --> 00:12:03.350
where I cannot change anything anymore.

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:04.600
I can just shorten the lengths-

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:05.640
So you're not one of the ones

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:07.084
who changes things the night before?

00:12:07.084 --> 00:12:08.910
No, but in couture also, you can't.

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:10.140
Okay.

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:12.930
Our interview takes place
in the recently restored

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.170
Balenciaga couture salon,

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:19.710
where tomorrow, in a show
with just 128 attendees,

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:22.810
including Kanye West, Lewis Hamilton

00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:26.496
and Kering chairman and
CEO Francois-Henri Pinault,

00:12:26.496 --> 00:12:29.610
Demna will pay homage
to the brand's history,

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:33.513
underscoring, the intimacy
and rarity of haute couture.

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:37.963
So I wanted to start with
where we are right now.

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:42.340
Can you tell me what this place signifies

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:44.380
to the house of Balenciaga?

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:46.640
This actual space salon,
where we're sitting,

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:50.860
is where the couture clients would come

00:12:50.860 --> 00:12:53.710
during the Cristobal Balenciaga era.

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:55.110
Originally it was the salon.

00:12:55.110 --> 00:12:55.943
It was the salon,

00:12:55.943 --> 00:12:59.480
this is the place where the
founder of this house would come

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:03.540
and create his legacy that
we're living through today.

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:05.010
It's symbolic, I don't know,

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:09.600
it's symbolic for my showing
respect to the legacy

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:12.120
that I'm lucky enough
to be in the middle of.

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:14.240
And now the couture has been in making

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:16.163
for over one year almost.

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:19.720
And it's the first live event
that we're going to have,

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:21.440
even if the audience is going to be

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:23.210
quite restricted and small.

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:26.733
What is your vision
for Balenciaga couture?

00:13:28.150 --> 00:13:30.310
I would say rather than my vision,

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:35.310
my mission in couture is to
actually give it a second life.

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:37.980
I heard people saying couture
is dead, who needs it?

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:40.400
You know, it's completely useless.

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:43.593
But I'm afraid of what's
going to be in 20, 30 years.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:47.300
After bursting onto the fashion scene

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:49.490
with the brand collective Vetements,

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:53.060
Demna took the helm of Balenciaga in 2015

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:55.740
as part of a radical modernization,

00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:57.490
including the Triple S,

00:13:57.490 --> 00:14:00.250
which set afire the ugly sneaker trend

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:02.450
and helped Balenciaga surpass

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:05.940
a billion dollars in revenue in 2019,

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:08.860
spawning a thousand copycats.

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:12.160
His fashion shows have been
as disruptive as his designs,

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:14.890
set in an enormous television studio

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:18.500
on the outskirts of the
Peripherique in Paris.

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:22.470
He's cast runway models
outside the traditional mold.

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:25.070
I like the fact that there's
boys at a couture show,

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:26.950
that's on your show, right?

00:14:26.950 --> 00:14:30.960
And the Balenciaga couture show
this week will feature men,

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:33.300
women, and non-binary people,

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:36.050
something that has not been
part of traditional couture

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:40.490
Karl Lagerfeld occasionally
featured men's couture

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:42.070
on his Chanel runways,

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:45.140
but it was not made available for sale.

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:47.140
I feel like talking about gender

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:49.560
is like talking about the
day before yesterday's news,

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:51.160
you know, we shouldn't
even be talking about it.

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:52.940
We should just be living it, in fashion.

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:56.570
And for me, the fact that
you saw boys and girls

00:14:56.570 --> 00:14:59.670
and non-binary people in my cast.

00:14:59.670 --> 00:15:01.970
Giving couture to men, for example,

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:03.300
for me they had to pay back some.

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:04.560
And so I wanted them to wear heels,

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:06.070
and to feel how it is to wear heels

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:09.513
and to see what it does
to different type of guys.

00:15:10.870 --> 00:15:12.210
Does some of this motivation,

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:15.710
to bring this into kind of
mainstream fashion and culture,

00:15:15.710 --> 00:15:18.280
come from having grown up in Georgia

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:22.030
during the time of the Soviet Union?

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:24.740
That wasn't a place where conversations

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:29.320
around gender identity, homosexuality-

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:32.346
So, I think there is a direct
link, for me personally,

00:15:32.346 --> 00:15:35.546
with the culture in which I
grew and don't ask, don't tell,

00:15:35.546 --> 00:15:37.470
I mean, officially there
are no gays in Georgia,

00:15:37.470 --> 00:15:41.180
you know, so obviously
it was a taboo subject.

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:45.180
I mean, I've been more
terrorized by homophobia

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:46.470
than the actual civil war,

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:47.970
so I feel like it is in me,

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:50.120
it's something that I have to do.

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:53.470
It's almost like a therapy
in a way to do that.

00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:55.770
And since fashion is
the mirror of our times,

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:58.620
I think it is kind of
part of my job, almost.

00:15:58.620 --> 00:15:59.750
Yeah.

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:01.540
What's your take on what we should learn

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:05.803
from the last 16 months of pause?

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:09.530
You know, I like to raise
questions through my work,

00:16:09.530 --> 00:16:12.380
you know, sustainability,
like raising questions

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:13.890
about consumption in general.

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:15.580
I mean, it's kind of ironic,

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:17.040
because I'm in the middle of the system

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:19.330
that encourages the consumption.

00:16:19.330 --> 00:16:21.550
But what I'm trying to do,
bringing couture to it,

00:16:21.550 --> 00:16:23.110
is actually start a conversation

00:16:23.110 --> 00:16:24.940
about thinking twice about consumption.

00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:28.110
I think, you know, couture for me

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:30.620
has something very modern in its concept

00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:33.400
because we only produce
what somebody orders.

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:36.340
So this is like the most
sustainable way of consuming,

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:39.000
but obviously the industry
cannot live on that.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:41.680
But I think it is also about
finding that balance, you know,

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:44.130
this pandemic made me, before pandemic,

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:45.050
I never thought about it.

00:16:45.050 --> 00:16:47.280
I was like a hamster in a
wheel just making stuff,

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:48.390
and making it and making it.

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:53.390
But the irony is, you need
to sell mountains of sneakers

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:58.280
in order to be able to sustain
both this couture collection

00:16:58.280 --> 00:16:59.380
and the business, right?

00:16:59.380 --> 00:17:00.600
You called it balance.

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:02.780
I think it is balance,
I think it's balance,

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:06.360
and it's also understanding
what people want from fashion.

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:08.140
I think that's the good starting point,

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:09.530
because they don't want all of that stuff

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:10.490
that is being produced.

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:12.470
They don't need it, they don't want it.

00:17:12.470 --> 00:17:14.050
And to be honest, they
don't care about it.

00:17:14.050 --> 00:17:18.100
Fashion has become too busy
to notice that, you know.

00:17:18.100 --> 00:17:21.390
The industry, and I feel that
pandemic gave that, somehow,

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:24.960
possibility to take a step
back and to kind of look at it

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:27.790
from objectively and to understand

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:29.140
you don't need all of that.

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:33.740
Recognizing the impact
fashion has on the planet

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:35.760
is a step forward.

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:38.060
But the scale of the problem is vast,

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:41.840
and the necessary change
can feel intimidating.

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:45.330
Some designers prefer to work
outside of the big houses,

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:47.490
turning down lucrative offers

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:49.763
instead to focus on their own brands.

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:54.690
One such designer is the
rising star of Paris fashion,

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:57.376
whose studio in the 19th arrondissement

00:17:57.376 --> 00:17:59.440
is bursting with old garments

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:01.340
that she repurposes to create

00:18:01.340 --> 00:18:03.363
her highly acclaimed collections.

00:18:04.347 --> 00:18:05.180
Wow.

00:18:05.180 --> 00:18:08.080
I met with Marine Serre to get her take

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:10.520
on how the pandemic is changing fashion

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:15.160
and to learn if that change
is happening fast enough.

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:17.330
So this is our upcycle room,

00:18:17.330 --> 00:18:20.440
where we are making garment
from regenerated fabric.

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:23.220
We take these from
warehouses, it's all waste,

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:25.620
and already used garment.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:27.360
But do they just sell it in bulk?

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:30.720
Yeah, so they have like tons
and tons and tons of garment,

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:32.150
when you enter into this place,

00:18:32.150 --> 00:18:35.210
you realize also how fashion industry

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:39.130
have been like producing
so much the last 50 years.

00:18:39.130 --> 00:18:41.660
So basically, this is our past, I mean,

00:18:41.660 --> 00:18:44.010
this is the past of
fashion and we are like,

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:48.110
taking piece of it and selecting into that

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:49.220
to make new garments.

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:51.480
And then no garment is
the same either, right?

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:54.500
Because every piece is different.

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:55.920
All of them, they are unique.

00:18:55.920 --> 00:18:59.140
And the only thing that repeats
is the process of making.

00:18:59.140 --> 00:19:00.560
But indeed the piece itself,

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:03.710
like if this will be on
the back of your shirt,

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:05.450
all of the shirt would be different.

00:19:05.450 --> 00:19:06.520
Right.

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:08.210
It was Marine's grandfather,

00:19:08.210 --> 00:19:10.680
a collector and secondhand dealer,

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:13.233
who first taught her the value of vintage.

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:17.910
Looking at fashion through an
ethical and sustainable lens

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.910
allowed Marine to foresee
many of the issues

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:24.003
that the industry is only
just starting to wake up to.

00:19:25.100 --> 00:19:26.750
You know, and you clearly feel very deeply

00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:28.350
about the state of the world.

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:32.620
You think deeply about the
climate and the environment,

00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:35.290
but you're now saying you feel optimistic.

00:19:35.290 --> 00:19:37.540
Well, I think before it was more about

00:19:37.540 --> 00:19:39.779
ringing the bell, alarming people,

00:19:39.779 --> 00:19:42.310
because like in the fashion industry,

00:19:42.310 --> 00:19:46.350
no one was looking at that
and I was also working

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:47.183
for this industry,

00:19:47.183 --> 00:19:51.817
so I do not want it to be
a part of just being there

00:19:51.817 --> 00:19:53.750
and making one more skirt,

00:19:53.750 --> 00:19:56.120
you know, I was hoping
that maybe, you know,

00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:58.100
some other people of the fashion industry

00:19:58.100 --> 00:19:59.380
were thinking about that too.

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:01.680
We really have to think about, yeah,

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:03.560
the environment that we are living in

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:05.840
and the planet that we
are destroying every day.

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:07.780
So if I'm there, I have to,

00:20:07.780 --> 00:20:11.720
at least making a small change.

00:20:11.720 --> 00:20:15.110
So what is luxury to you and
what is that luxury customer

00:20:15.110 --> 00:20:18.220
that you are appealing to,
what are they looking for?

00:20:18.220 --> 00:20:20.590
I believe that you can
feel it when you touch it.

00:20:20.590 --> 00:20:23.130
I think people that are in
fashion for long, they will know,

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:25.440
you know how this thing is stitch.

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:27.550
And even if you don't
know like this old thing,

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:29.420
you will feel it, like for me,

00:20:29.420 --> 00:20:32.540
it's really joyful when
I take a cushion cover

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:36.730
and then I can transform it
into a shirt that look cool.

00:20:36.730 --> 00:20:38.960
I'm like, yeah, that's nice.

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:41.290
What is the goal of the company?

00:20:41.290 --> 00:20:43.290
I think the goal of the company

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:48.090
is to question the fashion industry,

00:20:48.090 --> 00:20:50.670
try to resolve some of the question.

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:53.660
I mean, basically every
day we are doing that

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:56.880
and it's to make an ecosystem,

00:20:56.880 --> 00:21:00.163
a new ecosystem kind of exist.

00:21:01.430 --> 00:21:05.170
A fashion business does not
succeed on aesthetics alone.

00:21:05.170 --> 00:21:06.170
Now more than ever,

00:21:06.170 --> 00:21:09.870
those designs need to be
rooted in clear values.

00:21:09.870 --> 00:21:11.750
Sustainability, for me,

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:14.510
it has become a very
personal thing, really,

00:21:14.510 --> 00:21:17.250
just maybe doing couture is my way

00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:21.393
taking a minute of silence,
almost, to reflect.

00:21:22.250 --> 00:21:25.200
This is why the show will
be in a full silence.

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:26.033
No music?

00:21:26.033 --> 00:21:27.040
There will be no music,
this is the first time,

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:29.770
I never showed my work in silence.

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:30.603
Wow.

00:21:35.630 --> 00:21:38.310
That minute of silence, it's very symbolic

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:41.010
from what I just told you
about fashion as an industry,

00:21:41.010 --> 00:21:43.310
all the white noise,
things that don't matter.

00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:46.970
I've seen designers injecting

00:21:46.970 --> 00:21:49.310
more of their personal
values into their designs

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:52.840
and asking provocative
questions about fashion

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:54.730
and its role in the world.

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:56.870
Marine and Demna are asking how fashion

00:21:56.870 --> 00:22:00.850
can more positively impact
the people in our communities,

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:04.553
the societies we live in,
and the planet we all share.

